        <m>1</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>8</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>f(x)</m> is the solution to <m>f'(x)=0</m>. The derivative is<m>f'(x)=2x-8</m>. Setting this equal to zero and solving for <m>x</m> gives <m>x=4</m>. Evaluating <m>f(4)</m> yields the critical value -8.



Evaluating <m>f(0)</m> and <m>f(8)</m>, we find that each is equal to 8.



The extreme values must occur at critical points or at the endpoints of the interval. Since the value at the critical point is smaller than the value at the endpoints, the value of <m>f(4)</m> is a minimum, and the value of <m>f(0)</m> (or <m>f(8)</m> since they are equal) is a maximum.



There are no critical points in the interval <m>[0,1]</m>, so the extreme values lie at the endpoints of the interval. Computation yields <m>f(0)=8</m>,<m>f(1)=1</m>, so the minimum is 1 and the maximum is 8.


</solution>
